                <vector at="v36" v="(-0.306,0.306)" tail="(1.5,1.5)" stroke="#3c6fbf" thickness="2"/>
                <point at="q36" p="(1.5,1.5)" size="2" stroke="#8b1a2b" fill="none"/>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">yes</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">yes</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">no</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">yes</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">no</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes"><strcmp case="insensitive">no</strcmp><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></test>
        <test><strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review2-solutions">
        The plane is simply connected, so the integral is path independent
        exactly when <m>\partial P/\partial y = \partial Q/\partial x</m>.
      </p>
      <p component="review2-solutions">
        Field <m>A</m> is <m>\nabla\left(\frac{x^2+y^2}{2}\right)</m> and
        both derivatives are <m>0</m>: yes. Field <m>B</m> is
        <m>\nabla(xy)</m>, with both derivatives equal to <m>1</m>: yes.
        Field <m>D</m> is constant, hence
        <m>\nabla\left(x+\frac{y}{2}\right)</m>: yes.
      </p>
      <p component="review2-solutions">
        For <m>C</m>, <m>\partial P/\partial y = 1</m> while
        <m>\partial Q/\partial x = 0</m>: no. For <m>E</m> they are
        <m>0</m> and <m>1</m>: no. For <m>F</m> they are <m>-1</m> and
        <m>1</m>: no.
      </p>
      <p component="review2-solutions">
        The pictures agree. <m>A</m>, <m>B</m> and <m>D</m> have no
        circulation: follow any closed loop and the gains cancel the losses.
        <m>F</m> visibly swirls once around the origin, and <m>C</m> and
        <m>E</m> shear, so a rectangular loop picks up more along one side
        than it gives back along the opposite one.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="ex-r2p-sphere-minus-sphere">
    <title>Volume Between Two Spheres in Spherical Coordinates</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="volume" context="number">
        <de-expression>pi*a^3/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the volume of the region outside the sphere
        <m>\rho = <eval obj="a"/>\cos\phi</m> and inside the half sphere
        <m>\rho = <eval obj="a"/></m>, with
        <m>\phi \in \left[0, \frac{\pi}{2}\right]</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Volume <m>=</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="volume" width="14"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="volume.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(volume.value({})))" max="volume.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(volume.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review2-solutions">
        In spherical coordinates,
      </p>
      <me component="review2-solutions">
        V = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi/2}\!\!
        \int_{<eval obj="a"/>\cos\phi}^{<eval obj="a"/>}
        \rho^2\sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
        = \frac{2\pi\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)^3}{3}
        \int_0^{\pi/2}\left(1 - \cos^3\phi\right)\sin\phi\,d\phi .
      </me>
      <p component="review2-solutions">
        With <m>c = \cos\phi</m> the remaining integral is
        <m>\int_0^1 \left(1 - c^3\right) dc = \tfrac34</m>, so
        <m>V = \frac{2\pi\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)^3}{3}\cdot\frac34
        = \frac{\pi\left(<eval obj="a"/>\right)^3}{2}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r2p-spherical-shell">
    <title>A Triple Integral Over a Spherical Shell</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="r0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="gap" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="r1" context="number">
        <de-expression>r0+gap</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="radial" context="number">
        <de-expression>(r1^3-r0^3)/3</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="value" context="number">
        <de-expression>pi*(r1^3-r0^3)/3</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate, in spherical coordinates, the triple integral of
        <m>f(\rho,\theta,\phi) = \cos\phi</m> over the region
        <m>0 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>,
        <m>0 \le \phi \le \frac{\pi}{2}</m>,
        <m><eval obj="r0"/> \le \rho \le <eval obj="r1"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> The <m>\rho</m>-integral separates from the
        rest. Evaluate it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\int_{<eval obj="r0"/>}^{<eval obj="r1"/>}
        \rho^2\,d\rho =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="radial" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Evaluate the whole integral.
      </p>
      <p>
        Integral <m>=</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="value" width="14"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="radial.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(radial.value({})))" max="radial.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(radial.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="value.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(value.value({})))" max="value.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(value.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review2-solutions">
        Because the integrand and the volume element both factor, the
        triple integral splits into a product:
      </p>
      <me component="review2-solutions">
        \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi/2}\!\!
        \int_{<eval obj="r0"/>}^{<eval obj="r1"/>}
        \left(\cos\phi\right)\rho^2\sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
        = \left(\int_0^{2\pi} d\theta\right)
          \left(\int_{<eval obj="r0"/>}^{<eval obj="r1"/>}\rho^2 d\rho\right)
          \left(\int_0^{\pi/2}\cos\phi\sin\phi\,d\phi\right).
      </me>
      <p component="review2-solutions">
        These are <m>2\pi</m>,
        <m>\frac{\left(<eval obj="r1"/>\right)^3
        - \left(<eval obj="r0"/>\right)^3}{3}</m>, and <m>\tfrac12</m>,
        so the integral equals
        <m>\frac{\pi\left(\left(<eval obj="r1"/>\right)^3
        - \left(<eval obj="r0"/>\right)^3\right)}{3}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r2p-gaussian-shell">
    <title>An Exponential Integral Between Two Spheres</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="2" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="gap" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-expression>a+gap</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="asq" context="number">
        <de-expression>a^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="bsq" context="number">
        <de-expression>b^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="value" context="number">
        <de-expression>2*pi*(exp(-asq)-exp(-bsq))</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use spherical coordinates to evaluate the triple integral
      </p>
      <me>
        \iiint_E \frac{e^{-\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)}}
        {\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}}\,dV ,
      </me>
      <p>
        where <m>E</m> is the region bounded by the spheres
        <m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = <eval obj="asq"/></m> and
        <m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = <eval obj="bsq"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Answer <m>=</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="value" width="14"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="value.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(value.value({})))" max="value.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(value.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="review2-solutions">
        The spheres have radii <m><eval obj="a"/></m> and
        <m><eval obj="b"/></m>. In spherical coordinates,
      </p>
      <me component="review2-solutions">
        \iiint_E \frac{e^{-\rho^2}}{\rho}\,dV
        = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi}\!\!
          \int_{<eval obj="a"/>}^{<eval obj="b"/>}
          \frac{e^{-\rho^2}}{\rho}\,\rho^2\sin\phi
          \,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
        = 4\pi\int_{<eval obj="a"/>}^{<eval obj="b"/>}
          \rho\,e^{-\rho^2}\,d\rho .
      </me>
      <p component="review2-solutions">
        With <m>w = \rho^2</m> this is
        <m>4\pi\left[-\tfrac12 e^{-\rho^2}\right]_{<eval obj="a"/>}^{<eval obj="b"/>}
        = 2\pi\left(e^{-<eval obj="asq"/>} - e^{-<eval obj="bsq"/>}\right)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
